  "worked_solution": "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},\u2026,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},\u2026,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\u2261{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x\u2261{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,\u22ee \\\\ x\u2261{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}\u22ef{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system I since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system III since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
